      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
                "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the first text of the Philosophy section, we studied Aristotle's understanding of knowledge and observed that it grows as we study, reaching the point where we realize it is not possible to know everything (<htcite0>).\n\nBeyond this conclusion, we learned that as we acquire more knowledge, we realize that having many certainties can actually indicate that we still do not know many things (<htcite1>).\n\nThese facts lead us to some questions:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "3b95809f-e017-4f2c-9c30-bc3db69ce7c5",
                     "text": "Plato",
                     "page": "pp. 15",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1899",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9c5cd96b-da2e-48b3-a88e-a6556c9973a7",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                      "page": "I understand it as...",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown or html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
                "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: On limits</b><br /><br />Can we learn everything in school?<br /><br />Or does school only provide us with the foundations to continue learning?<br /><br />Upon graduating, does a person realize that there is much to learn? Or do most people think they have reached the final goal?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_game",
         "text": [
            {
                "text": "### A Russian game\n\nAs we explore a bit about knowledge, we learn about Doctor Francisco A. González Redondo's proposal, according to which, in school, students are not introduced to mathematics, but rather to a pre-mathematics and a proto-mathematics, which constitute the initial stages of learning. In one of his lectures, this concept is even generalized to other areas of knowledge (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>; <htcite3>).\n\n#### The answers\n\nFrancisco A. González Redondo's understanding is entirely in agreement with the understanding Aristotle reached millennia ago. It indicates to us that, as we study, there is no possibility of mastering everything. On this path of knowledge, we begin by making small discoveries from our own body, which would correspond to the pre phase. Next, as we grow in knowledge, we encompass the previous knowledge, incorporating it, but still without full knowledge (the proto phase). Finally, we begin to actually understand a given subject and, at this stage, we realize that there will always be a horizon to be explored.\n\nThis understanding allows us to answer the questions from **Reflection 1**: with the advancement of knowledge in school, we go through the first steps to understand something greater ahead, which will allow us to better understand the universe around us.\n\n#### The Matryoshka (матрёшка)\n\nWhat we are studying in this text can be represented by a toy created in Russia: the Matryoshka doll (*матрёшка*), as shown in **Graph 1**.",
